<main id="content"><header class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Umicom Framework &amp; Applications</p><h1>Read a compiler diagnostic</h1><p class="lead">Use Framework to separate a Notes compiler message into its source location and explanation.</p></header>
<h2>Read a useful compiler message</h2><p>Suppose a Notes program is missing a semicolon. The compiler reports the source file and location. Studio needs those separate fields to show a useful problem and return to the code. A Windows drive letter contains a colon, so splitting the message at every colon is not a correct parser.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Source location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Public build-parser interface</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/include/umicom/build/parser.h"><code>include/umicom/build/parser.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Build diagnostic data</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/include/umicom/build/diagnostic.h"><code>include/umicom/build/diagnostic.h</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Shared compiler scanner</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/src/diagnostics/compiler_parser.c"><code>src/diagnostics/compiler_parser.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Build compatibility facade</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/src/build/parser.c"><code>src/build/parser.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Source navigation</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/src/diagnostic_ui/navigation.c"><code>src/diagnostic_ui/navigation.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Regression checks</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/tests/test_compiler_source_workflow.c"><code>tests/test_compiler_source_workflow.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Complete example</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/examples/build_diagnostics/main.c"><code>examples/build_diagnostics/main.c</code></a></td></tr><tr><td>Independent CMake example</td><td><a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation/umicom-framework/blob/main/examples/build_diagnostics/CMakeLists.txt"><code>examples/build_diagnostics/CMakeLists.txt</code></a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>The complete program</h2><p>This code is included from the existing example file during the documentation build. It is not a separate copy maintained by the website.</p><pre><code>@UMICOM_SITE_DIAGNOSTIC_EXAMPLE@</code></pre><h2>Follow the data</h2><p><code>line</code> is borrowed input: the parser reads it during the call. <code>problem</code> is output storage owned by this program. The call returns a <code>UmiStatus</code>. Check it before using the fields. A success result means the supported message has been parsed; it does not mean the compiler build succeeded.</p><p><code>problem.file</code> identifies the reported source; <code>line</code> and <code>column</code> hold its location. These are compiler coordinates, not necessarily the visual character columns shown around tabs or non-ASCII text. The default example checks its expected location before returning success.</p>
<h2>Build against your installed SDK</h2><p>The examples below use <code>C:/umicom/installed/development</code> or <code>$HOME/umicom/installed/development</code> for an SDK you have already installed. Substitute the actual SDK prefix; do not rename an existing installation merely to match this example.</p><h3>Windows</h3><pre><code>Set-Location "C:\umicom\Umicom-Applications"
$env:Path = "C:\msys64\ucrt64\bin;$env:Path"
cmake -S framework/examples/build_diagnostics -B build/notes-diagnostics -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="C:/umicom/installed/development" -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER="C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/gcc.exe" -DCMAKE_MAKE_PROGRAM="C:/msys64/ucrt64/bin/ninja.exe"
cmake --build build/notes-diagnostics
ctest --test-dir build/notes-diagnostics --no-tests=error --output-on-failure
&amp; ".\build\notes-diagnostics\umicom-notes-diagnostics.exe"</code></pre><h3>Linux</h3><pre><code>cd "$HOME/umicom/Umicom-Applications"
cmake -S framework/examples/build_diagnostics -B build/notes-diagnostics -G Ninja -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH="$HOME/umicom/installed/development"
cmake --build build/notes-diagnostics
ctest --test-dir build/notes-diagnostics --no-tests=error --output-on-failure
./build/notes-diagnostics/umicom-notes-diagnostics</code></pre><p>The example links <code>Umicom::build</code>. It does not compile another parsing implementation into the application.</p><h2>Expected output</h2><pre><code>Source: C:/Umicom Notes/src/main.c
Line: 8
Column: 5
Message: expected ';' before return</code></pre><h2>Try a warning</h2><p>Pass a quoted diagnostic string as the first argument, changing the severity to <code>warning</code> and the position to <code>12:3</code>. Check the output. Then pass <code>0 errors and 0 warnings</code>; a summary is not a source diagnostic, so this program should report that no complete diagnostic was found and return a failure status.</p><p>The scanner supports specific compiler formats; it is not a universal protocol for all tools and localised messages. Read its tests before adding another format. Keep malformed and overlong input tests alongside the normal examples.</p>
</main><footer><strong>Umicom Foundation</strong><p>Learn from the source. Build with shared components.</p><a href="../contributing.html">Improve these guides</a> · <a href="https://github.com/umicom-foundation">GitHub organisation</a></footer></body></html>
